    {
      "id": "DET-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Residue detection, Bockstein fibers and the homology bound",
      "anchor": "inputs-detecting-count",
      "statement": "Equality of all scaled evaluation coordinates implies that the global powered norm ratio is a qth power, so the chosen set S injects into the finite collection of evaluation tuples. It has order at most q^r₂ T_(2e+1)T_(2e), with T_j torsion order in the integral homology of GL_(4e+6)(O_L′).",
      "hypotheses": "All tested reductions avoid finite denominators and are surjective from O_L′. A primitive qth root lies in F. Degree-one Chebotarev is required. The upper bound uses stable integral torsion, primitive/equivariant evaluation families, m odd and m<r₂ for the odd-degree filtered-rank theorem. The product target may have infinitely many coordinates; only the attained finite set is counted.",
      "definitions": [
        "residue degree one",
        "power detection",
        "Bockstein connecting map",
        "group homology",
        "odd stabilized rank",
        "finite fibers"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-detecting-cap"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "DET-01",
        "DET-02",
        "STB-02"
      ],
      "correspondence": "In the global detection proof choose a radical; roots of unity remain distinct away from ℓ. Degree-one Frobenii realize every Galois action on it, and all fix it. In homology the boundary of an integral lift equals q times a lower cycle. Its class depends only on the reduced cycle, is killed by q, and differences in one fiber lift integrally. The zSeq comparison, rank theorem and two stable torsion groups bound its values. NumThree cancels q^r₂ after comparison with the character count.",
